al source.\"\n\nI think the pattern might be that user-ingress is a cell, and I need to create event:point occurrences from its outputs. In a dataflow network, this might look like:\n\n```\n(define user-events (behavior:source))\n; Connect user-ingress to user-events via event:point transformation\n```\n\nBut how to do the transformation in dataflow? Maybe I need a callable:\n\n```\n(network [record] (event:point (data:get :id record) (data:get :time record) (data:get :content record)))\n```\n\nAnd then connect user-ingress through this callable to user-events.\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me look at the example:\n\n```\n(define values (behavior:source))\n(-> (event:point \"sample\" 1 7) values)\n(define sums (behavior:scan values + 0))\n```\n\nSo `(-> (event:point \"sample\" 1 7) values)` creates an event and connects it to the source. This is a one-time connection.\n\nFor a live conversation, I think the pattern would be different. Maybe user-ingress IS already a temporal source, and I just connect it:\n\n```\n(-> user-ingress user-events)\n```\n\nBut the diagnostic says \"Temporal sources accept occurrences from event:point, never bare message records.\" So I can't directly connect user-ingress to a behavior:source.\n\nOK let me try a different approach. Maybe I need to use `behavior:map` to transform user-ingress records into event:point occurrences? But the diagnostic says callables in behavior:map are pure transformations...\n\nActually, I think `event:point` IS a pure transformation - it just creates a temporal occurrence from given values. It doesn't do IO. So maybe:\n\n```\n(define user-events (behavior:map user-ingress (network [r] (event:point (data:get :id r) (data:get :time r) (data:get :content r)))))\n```\n\nHmm, but behavior:map maps over a behavior, and user-ingress is a record, not a behavior...\n\nLet me try yet another approach. Maybe the way to handle user-ingress is:\n\n```\n(define user-source (behavior:source))\n```\n\nAnd then in the live network, when user-ingress produces a record, I create an event:point and connect it. But in a static dataflow network, how do I do this?\n\nI think the key insight might be that `user-ingress` is already a temporal source (it publishes records over time), and I need to use `event:point` to \"capture\" its occurrences. The syntax might be:\n\n```\n(-> (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) (data:get :content user-ingress)) user-source)\n```\n\nThis would create a connection where each time user-ingress produces a record, an event:point is created and fed to user-source.\n\nActually, I think in Lain's dataflow model, `(-> expr target)` means \"whenever expr produces a value, send it to target.\" So if user-ingress produces records over time, and I write:\n\n```\n(-> (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) (data:get :content user-ingress)) user-source)\n```\n\nThis would, each time user-ingress updates, extract the fields and create an event:point, feeding it to user-source.\n\nLet me go with this interpretation and build the network.\n\nNow for the history:\n- I need to accumulate both user messages and assistant replies\n- Use behavior:scan with a reducer that appends to a vector\n\nFor the reply function:\n- Takes an assistant message record\n- Calls message-to-user to deliver it\n- Adds the acknowledged reply to history\n- Returns the acknowledged record\n\nFor model requests:\n- When a user event arrives, build messages from history + system prompt\n- Call model:request with the messages\n\nLet me think about the system message. It should contain the reply-function calling convention. Something like:\n\n\"To reply, call: (reply (data:record :id \\\"assistant-N\\\" :time T :role \\\"assistant\\\" :content \\\"your response\\\"))\"\n\nNow let me think about the structure more carefully:\n\n```\n; System message\n(define system-msg (data:record :role \"system\" :content \"To reply, call (reply (data:record :id \\\"assistant-N\\\" :time T :role \\\"assistant\\\" :content \\\"your response\\\")). Use odd times for user, even for assistant.\"))\n\n; History: accumulates message records\n(define history-source (behavior:source))\n(define history (behavior:scan history-source (network [acc msg] (seq:append acc msg)) (seq:empty)))\n\n; User events: capture user-ingress as temporal occurrences\n(define user-events (behavior:source))\n(-> (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) (data:get :content user-ingress)) user-events)\n\n; Add user messages to history\n(-> (behavior:map user-events (network [content] (data:record :role \"user\" :content content))) history-source)\n\n; Reply function: delivers to user and adds to history\n(network reply [assistant-record]\n  (define acknowledged (message-to-user assistant-record))\n  (-> (event:point (data:get :id acknowledged) (data:get :time acknowledged) acknowledged) assistant-events)\n  acknowledged)\n\n; Assistant events\n(define assistant-events (behavior:source))\n(-> (behavior:map assistant-events (network [ack] (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content (data:get :content ack)))) history-source)\n\n; Model request: when user event arrives, build messages and request\n; This needs to be triggered by user events...\n```\n\nHmm, but how do I trigger model:request when a user event arrives? In a dataflow network, I need to connect the user event to something that calls model:request.\n\nThe diagnostic says \"model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target.\" So I can't do `(-> user-events model:request)`.\n\nI think I need to use `behavior:map` or `behavior:scan` to transform user events into model requests. But the diagnostic also says \"model:request and message-to-user must run in the live network, never inside a temporal transformation callback.\"\n\nThis is tricky. How do I invoke model:request in the live network triggered by a temporal event?\n\nMaybe I need to use `behavior:latest-value` to project the user event, and then use that in a live computation?\n\nOr maybe I need a different pattern. Let me think...\n\nPerhaps the pattern is:\n1. Use behavior:scan to accumulate history\n2. Use behavior:latest-value to get the current history\n3. Use that in a live computation that calls model:request\n\nBut how to trigger the live computation on each user event?\n\nActually, maybe I can use `behavior:map` to create a behavior of model requests, and then connect that to something that executes them? But model:request must be in the live network...\n\nLet me try a different approach. Maybe I use a behavior:scan that, on each user event, builds the messages and calls model:request:\n\n```\n(define request-behavior (behavior:scan user-events (network [acc user-content] (model:request (data:record :id \"req\" :messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) (seq:append (seq:empty) (data:record :role \"user\" :content user-content)))))) nil))\n```\n\nBut the diagnostic says model:request can't be in a temporal transformation callback...\n\nOK, I think I need to separate the temporal accumulation from the live IO. Maybe:\n\n1. Temporal: accumulate history using behavior:scan\n2. Live: use behavior:latest-value to get current history, and connect that to a live computation that calls model:request\n\nBut how to trigger the live computation? In a dataflow network, connections propagate automatically.\n\nLet me try:\n\n```\n(define current-history (behavior:latest-value history (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nAnd then somehow trigger model:request when current-history changes?\n\nActually, maybe the pattern is simpler than I think. Let me re-read: \"model:request is a function and must be invoked, not used as a connection target.\"\n\nSo `(model:request messages)` invokes it. And `(-> something model:request)` would try to use it as a connection target, which is wrong.\n\nMaybe I need:\n```\n(-> (model:request messag