diagnostic says \"model:request and message-to-user must run in the live network, never inside a temporal transformation callback.\" A `(network ...)` callable is not a temporal transformation callback - it's a regular callable. So IO inside it should be fine.\n\nLet me also reconsider the `if` gate. `(data:equal? latest-user latest-user)` - this compares latest-user to itself. If latest-user is unknown, the comparison is unknown. If it's a string, it's true. This should work as a gate.\n\nBut actually, I just realized: `behavior:latest-value user-events` without a fallback returns the latest value of the behavior. If no events have occurred, it's unknown. Once an event occurs, it's the value of that event (the content string).\n\nSo `(data:equal? latest-user latest-user)` would be:\n- Unknown → unknown (suspended)\n- \"hello\" → true (proceed)\n\nThis works as a gate.\n\nBut there's another issue: after the first user event, `latest-user` is always known (it's the content of the latest user event). So the gate is always open after the first event. That's correct - we want to request the model on every user event.\n\nBut wait, the `messages` computation depends on `current-history`, which includes the user message. And the user message is added to history via `behavior:map user-events ...`. So when a new user event arrives:\n1. `latest-user` updates (gate opens)\n2. `behavior:map` creates a record and adds to history-source\n3. `history` scan updates\n4. `current-history` updates\n5. `messages` updates\n6. `model:request` fires\n\nThis should work in a dataflow network.\n\nOne concern: the `if` condition `(data:equal? latest-user latest-user)` is always true after the first event. So the gate is effectively always open after the first event. That's fine.\n\nBut what about the initial state? Before any user event:\n- `latest-user` is unknown\n- `data:equal? unknown unknown` is unknown\n- `if unknown ...` is unknown\n- `messages` is unknown\n- `model:request` is suspended\n\nAfter the first user event:\n- `latest-user` is \"hello\" (or whatever)\n- `data:equal? \"hello\" \"hello\"` is true\n- `if true ...` selects the yes branch\n- `messages` is [system, user1]\n- `model:request` fires\n\nAfter the model responds and the reply is delivered:\n- Assistant message is added to history\n- `current-history` updates\n- `messages` updates to [system, user1, assistant1]\n- But `model:request` doesn't fire again because no new user event arrived\n\nWait, actually, `messages` depends on `current-history`, which updates when the assistant message is added. So `model:request` would fire again! That's a problem.\n\nI need to gate the request on user events, not on history changes. Let me restructure:\n\nThe `messages` computation should only update when a user event arrives, not when an assistant message is added.\n\nOne way: use `behavior:scan` to build messages, triggered only by user events:\n\n```\n(define messages-behavior (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [acc content] \n    (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) \n                (seq:append acc (data:record :role \"user\" :content content))))\n  (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nBut this doesn't include assistant messages, and model:request can't be in a temporal callback.\n\nAnother way: use `behavior:latest-value` on a behavior that only updates on user events:\n\n```\n(define user-trigger (behavior:scan user-events (network [_ _] true) false))\n(define ready (behavior:latest-value user-trigger))\n```\n\nBut this starts as `false`, not unknown. So the gate would be closed initially (false → no branch), and open after the first event (true → yes branch). But it would also be open when assistant messages are added (because `ready` is still true).\n\nHmm, the issue is that `messages` depends on `current-history`, which updates on both user and assistant events. I need to decouple the request trigger from the history update.\n\nWhat if I use `behavior:latest-value` on a behavior that