ages` depends on `current-history`, which updates on both user and assistant events. I need to decouple the request trigger from the history update.\n\nWhat if I use `behavior:latest-value` on a behavior that snapshots the history at user event time?\n\n```\n(define request-history (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [_ _] (behavior:latest-value history (seq:empty)))\n  (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nNo, this doesn't work because `behavior:latest-value` inside a scan callback is a temporal transformation.\n\nOK, let me try a different approach. What if I use two separate behaviors:\n1. `user-events` for user messages\n2. `assistant-events` for assistant messages\nAnd build messages from both, but only trigger the request on user events?\n\n```\n(define user-history (behavior:scan user-events (network [acc content] (seq:append acc (data:record :role \"user\" :content content))) (seq:empty)))\n(define assistant-history (behavior:scan assistant-events (network [acc ack] (seq:append acc (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content (data:get :content ack)))) (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nBut interleaving them correctly is complex.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. In a dataflow network, when an assistant message is added to history, `current-history` updates, and `messages` updates. But `model:request` is connected to `messages` via `->`. So it would fire again.\n\nBut wait, does `model:request` fire every time its input changes? In a dataflow network, yes, connections propagate. So every time `messages` changes, `model:request` is called.\n\nThis means:\n1. User event → messages updates → model:request fires ✓\n2. Assistant reply → history updates → messages updates → model:request fires ✗ (unwanted)\n\nI need to prevent the second case. \n\nOne approach: make `messages` depend on a \"request trigger\" that only changes on user events:\n\n```\n(define request-trigger (behavior:scan user-events (network [acc _] (+ acc 1)) 0))\n(define trigger-value (behavior:latest-value request-trigger))\n```\n\nAnd use `trigger-value` in the messages computation:\n\n```\n(define messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) \n                              (seq:append (seq:empty) (data:record :role \"user\" :content (text:format \"trigger-%s\" trigger-value)))))\n```\n\nNo, this is hacky and wrong.\n\nLet me think about this differently. What if I use `behavior:latest-value` on a behavior that captures the history snapshot at user event time?\n\nActually, I think the cleanest solution is to use `behavior:scan` to build the messages, triggered by user events, and include the current history:\n\n```\n(define messages-behavior (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [acc content] \n    (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) \n                (seq:append (seq:empty) (data:record :role \"user\" :content content))))\n  (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nBut this doesn't include assistant messages. And I can't call model:request inside the scan.\n\nWhat if I use `behavior:latest-value` on messages-behavior, and then add history?\n\n```\n(define latest-messages (behavior:latest-value messages-behavior (seq:empty)))\n(define full-messages (seq:concat latest-messages current-history))\n```\n\nNo, this doesn't make sense.\n\nOK, let me try yet another approach. What if the history is built from BOTH user and assistant events, and the request is triggered by a separate mechanism?\n\n```\n(define history-source (behavior:source))\n(define history (behavior:scan history-source (network [acc msg] (seq:append acc msg)) (seq:empty)))\n(define current-history (behavior:latest-value history (seq:empty)))\n\n; User events go to history AND trigger request\n(-> (behavior:map user-events (network [content] (data:record :role \"user\" :content content))) history-source)\n\n; Assistant events go to history only\n(-> (behavior:map assistant-events (network [ack] (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content (data:get :content ack)))) history-source)\n\n; Request trigger: only fires on user events\n(define request-trigger (behavior:scan user-events (network [_ _] true) false))\n(define ready (behavior:latest-value request-trigger))\n\n; Messages: gated on user events\n(define messages (if ready \n                     (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) current-history)\n                     (seq:empty)))\n\n(-> (model:request (data:record :id \"req\" :messages messages)) model-output)\n```\n\nThe issue remains: when an assistant message is added to history, `current-history` updates, `messages` updates (because `ready` is true), and `model:request` fires again.\n\nTo prevent this, I need `messages` to NOT depend on `current-history` directly, but on a snapshot of history taken at user event time.\n\nWhat if I use `behavior:scan` to snapshot history at user event time?\n\n```\n(define request-history (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [_ _] current-history)\n  (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nBut `current-history` inside a scan callback... is this a temporal transformation? The diagnostic says \"A callable inside behavior:map or scan is evaluated as a pure transformation.\" So `current-history` would be evaluated as a pure transformation, which means it reads the current value of the cell. But in a temporal callback, this might not work correctly.\n\nActually, I think in a dataflow network, `current-history` is a cell. Reading it inside a scan callback would give the current value at the time the callback runs. But the diagnostic warns against IO in temporal callbacks, not against reading cells.\n\nLet me try:\n```\n(define request-history (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [_ _] current-history)\n  (seq:empty)))\n(define messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) (behavior:latest-value request-history (seq:empty))))\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me try a simpler approach.\n\nWhat if I just accept that model:request fires on every history change, but the model is smart enough to not respond to assistant messages? No, that's not how it works. model:request sends messages to the model, and the model responds.\n\nOK, let me try the simplest possible approach and see if it works:\n\n```\n(define system-msg (data:record :role \"system\" :content \"...\"))\n(define user-events (behavior:source))\n(define assistant-events (behavior:source))\n(define history-source (behavior:source))\n(define history (behavior:scan history-source (network [acc msg] (seq:append acc msg)) (seq:empty)))\n\n(-> (event:point (data:get :id user-ingress) (data:get :time user-ingress) (data:get :content user-ingress)) user-events)\n(-> (behavior:map user-events (network [c] (data:record :role \"user\" :content c))) history-source)\n(-> (behavior:map assistant-events (network [a] (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content (data:get :content a)))) history-source)\n\n(network reply [r] (let [ack (message-to-user r)] (event:point (data:get :id ack) (data:get :time ack) ack)))\n\n(define latest-user (behavior:latest-value user-events))\n(define current-history (behavior:latest-value history (seq:empty)))\n(define messages (if (data:equal? latest-user latest-user)\n                     (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) current-history)\n                     (seq:empty)))\n(-> (model:request (data:record :id \"req\" :messages messages)) model-output)\n```\n\nThe double-fire issue: when an assistant message is added to history, `current-history` changes, `messages` changes, and `model:request` fires. But `latest-user` hasn't changed, so the `if` condition is still true. So yes, it would fire.\n\nTo fix this, I need to make `messages` depend on `latest-user` in a way that it only changes when `latest-user` changes.\n\nWhat if I include `latest-user` in the messages computation?\n\n```\n(define messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) \n                              (seq:append (seq:empty) (data:record :role \"user\" :content latest-user))))\n```\n\nThis only changes when `latest-user` changes. But it doesn't include history.\n\nFor multi-turn, I need history. But I can't include history without causing double-fires.\n\nWhat if I use a different structure: instead of a single history, I use the user-events and assistant-events behaviors directly?\n\n```\n(define user-msgs (behavior:scan user-events (network [acc c] (seq:append acc (data:record :role \"user\" :content c))) (seq:empty)))\n(define assistant-msgs (behavior:scan assistant-events (network [acc a] (seq:append acc (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content (data:get :content a)))) (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nAnd then interleave them? But interleaving two vectors in a dataflow way is complex.\n\nActually, for a simple conversation, the messages are always: system, user1, assistant1, user2, assistant2, ...\n\nSo I could use a single scan that alternates:\n\n```\n(define messages-behavior (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [acc content] \n    (seq:concat acc (seq:append (seq:empty) (data:record :role \"user\" :content content))))\n  (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg)))\n```\n\nAnd then add assistant messages to this behavior? But I can't modify a behavior from outside.\n\nOK, I think the cleanest solution for the double-fire problem is to use `behavior:latest-value` on a behavior that only updates on user events, and use that as the trigger:\n\n```\n(define user-trigger (behavior:scan user-events (network [_ _] true) false))\n(define ready (behavior:latest-value user-trigger))\n```\n\nBut `ready` starts as `false` (the initial value of the scan). So the `if` would select the `no` branch initially. After the first user event, `ready` becomes `true`. After that, it stays `true`.\n\nThe problem is that `messages` depends on `current-history`, which updates on assistant events too. So even though `ready` doesn't change, `messages` changes because `current-history` changes.\n\nTo fix this, I need `messages` to NOT depend on `current-history` directly. Instead, I need a snapshot of history that only updates on user events.\n\nWhat if I use `behavior:scan` to snapshot history at user event time?\n\n```\n(define request-snapshot (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [_ _] current-history)\n  (seq:empty)))\n(define messages (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) (behavior:latest-value request-snapshot (seq:empty))))\n```\n\nBut `current-history` inside a scan callback... Let me check if this is allowed. The diagnostic says \"A callable inside behavior:map or scan is evaluated as a pure transformation.\" Reading a cell is a pure transformation (no IO). So this should be OK.\n\nBut wait, `current-history` is a cell. In a scan callback, reading a cell gives its current value. But the scan callback runs when a user event arrives. At that point, `current-history` should have the latest history (including the user message that just arrived, if the propagation order is right).\n\nActually, in a dataflow network, when a user event arrives:\n1. The user event is fed to user-events source\n2. behavior:map transforms it and adds to history-source\n3. history scan updates\n4. current-history updates\n5. The scan callback for request-snapshot runs, reading current", :finish-reason "length", :truncated? true, :request-id "setup"}, :support #{{:source #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "90b408ce-202c-3dcf-8c16-096049a4f308"}, :timestamp 1, :premises-status :active}}}}, :states #{{:source #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "90b408ce-202c-3dcf-8c16-096049a4f308"}, :timestamp 1, :premises-status :active}}}, [[:outer] #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "eaee3593-1a40-3876-9ea0-ce9e276467c5"}] {:gur.flat/recursive-closure? true, :compiler-2/declaration #propagators.network.Net{:graph {#propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a91e-cbd7-785e-8ae2-5a7145727a18"} #propagators.graph.Node{:inputs #{}, :outputs #{}}, #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid #uuid "01a1a91e-cbd7-785e-8ae2-5a7145727a19"} #propagators.graph.Node{:inputs #{}, :outputs #{}}, #propagators.ids.NodeId{:uuid