s added.\n\nOne way: use `behavior:scan` to build messages, triggered only by user events:\n\n```\n(define messages-behavior (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [acc content] \n    (seq:concat (seq:append (seq:empty) system-msg) \n                (seq:append acc (data:record :role \"user\" :content content))))\n  (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nBut this doesn't include assistant messages, and model:request can't be in a temporal callback.\n\nAnother way: use `behavior:latest-value` on a behavior that only updates on user events:\n\n```\n(define user-trigger (behavior:scan user-events (network [_ _] true) false))\n(define ready (behavior:latest-value user-trigger))\n```\n\nBut this starts as `false`, not unknown. So the gate would be closed initially (false → no branch), and open after the first event (true → yes branch). But it would also be open when assistant messages are added (because `ready` is still true).\n\nHmm, the issue is that `messages` depends on `current-history`, which updates on both user and assistant events. I need to decouple the request trigger from the history update.\n\nWhat if I use `behavior:latest-value` on a behavior that snapshots the history at user event time?\n\n```\n(define request-history (behavior:scan user-events \n  (network [_ _] (behavior:latest-value history (seq:empty)))\n  (seq:empty)))\n```\n\nNo, this doesn't work because `behavior:latest-value` inside a scan callback is a temporal transformation.\n\nOK, let me try a different approach. What if I use two separate behaviors:\n1. `user-events` for user messages\n2. `assistant-events` for assistant messages\nAnd build messages from both, but only trigger the request on user events?\n\n```\n(define user-history (behavior:scan user-events (network [acc content] (seq:append acc (data:record :role \"user\" :content content))) (seq:empty)))\n(define assistant-history (behavior:scan assistant-events (network [acc ack] (seq:append acc (data:record :role \"assistant\" :content (data:get :con